# TODO - Integrate the entire theoretical source pipeline

## Objective

PCI → PHY → DMA → 802.11 MAC → WPA2/CCMP → native AirPort. Resolve
state/locking ownership, data movement, failures, cleanup, suspend/resume and
reconnection in code. Remove placeholders and invented readiness indicators.
**Source completeness only; not physical connectivity.**

## Acceptance

- [ ] Implement/review the stated source and integrate on main.
- [ ] Join source paths without fake readiness, leaks or unsafe error recovery.
- [ ] Ground decisions in public code, published standards and SDK/KDK references.
- [ ] Record unresolved assumptions and fail-closed behavior.
- [ ] Complete with source/static evidence, NEVER requiring a physical Dell test.

## Required source deliverables

- [ ] Trace actual startup from SimpleATH::start through retained PCI/D0,
      supported SREV, validated board data, calibrated radio, mapped queues,
      interrupts, MAC/security and published native AirPort interface.
- [ ] Trace actual 2.4 GHz scan, WPA2 connect, outgoing OS packet, protected
      device TX, RX/decryption and incoming OS packet through implemented
      call sites. State the owner, queue, lifetime and error path at each step.
- [ ] Include every required implementation unit and dependency in the Xcode
      target. Normal supported paths must reach real implementations rather
      than NotImplemented returns, empty callbacks or unconditional #if 0.
- [ ] Review partial-start failure and every stop/reset/sleep/provider-loss
      ordering; demonstrate in source how stale work is rejected and DMA/key/
      packet resources retire safely. Trace reconnect after a fresh resume.
- [ ] Document remaining uncertainty with evidence. Required functionality
      cannot be declared complete by inserting a boolean readiness assertion
      or an always-rejecting safety gate; implement the supported normal path.

## Evidence

- Source commits, reference URLs/version/rights, static review and lint diagnostics.
- Theoretical completeness only; no build, device scan, packet logs or hardware claims.
